PE-ID-ALLOCATION.md section 3.1 reserves, so verbatim adoption still holds. Runtime types sit at 43/44/47. FLAG E, the one silent-capable collision in the whole contract, CONFIRMED AT THIS HEAD: TURBO2_0 is id 43 and our Q2_0_G128 is id 43, and block_turbo2_0 (ggml_half + qs[128/4] = 34 B / 128 values) is BYTE-IDENTICAL AND FIELD-IDENTICAL to block_q2_0_g128, so gguf_init's row-size check has nothing to separate them and the g128 metadata gate keys off a marker a turbo2 file would never carry - a serialized TURBO2_0 read as our g128 would dequantize wrongly and KEEP GOING (turbo2 is WHT-rotated Lloyd-Max, ours is plain scaled). THAT 'SILENT' VERDICT IS CODE-DERIVED, NOT CONTRACT-PROSE: read at recut/2026-08-16:src/llama-model-loader.cpp, llama_gguf_q2_0_g128_gate returns FALSE on `key < 0` (line 133-135) without throwing, and the only consumer is `if (q2_0_g128_enabled)` at :870 which skips the span check and llama_remap_q2_0_g128 entirely. Nothing on the false branch rejects id-43 tensors. gguf_init has already validated their spans against ggml_row_size(43) = 34 B / 128, which a turbo2_0 file matches exactly. So the file parses clean and our plain-scaled g128 kernel runs on rotated data. NOT TRIGGERED today, verified three ways at this head: no LLAMA_FTYPE_MOSTLY_TURBO2_0 in include/llama.h, no TURBO target in tools/quantize/quantize.cpp, no TURBO entry in gguf-py/gguf/constants.py. SENSITIVITY HALF, added r2 after checker finding F6 - three asserted ABSENCES are void as evidence until the query is shown able to HIT in those same files, so: all three paths exist and are non-trivial at that ref (1350 / 596 / 4973 lines), each one DOES carry the turbo family under its sibling names (LLAMA_FTYPE_MOSTLY_TQ3_1S = 43 in llama.h, a {\"TQ3_1S\", ...} row in quantize.cpp, TQ3_1S = 45 in constants.py), and `TURBO` is a live token elsewhere in the same tree (GGML_TYPE_TURBO2_0 = 43, GGML_OP_TURBO_WHT) - yet it returns ZERO in all three registries. The query can fire, the fil